BLAKE3
======

blake3*.c, blake3_impl.h and src/include/common/blake3.h are the official
BLAKE3 C implementation, version 1.8.2, from
https://github.com/BLAKE3-team/BLAKE3 (its c/ directory), used under its
CC0 1.0 Universal dedication (the project offers CC0, Apache 2.0, or
Apache 2.0 with LLVM exceptions), whose text is LICENSE.blake3 (upstream's
LICENSE_CC0).

They are kept as upstream wrote them, so a newer version can be dropped
in, but for one change: blake3.c and blake3_impl.h include
"common/blake3.h" where upstream includes "blake3.h".

The build compiles blake3.c, blake3_dispatch.c and blake3_portable.c
everywhere; on x86 also blake3_sse2.c, blake3_sse41.c, blake3_avx2.c and
blake3_avx512.c, each with the instructions it uses enabled (-msse2,
-msse4.1, -mavx2, -mavx512f -mavx512vl), and on AArch64 blake3_neon.c.
The build first checks the compiler can build each x86 variant's
intrinsics with its flags; one it can't is left out, and BLAKE3_NO_SSE2,
BLAKE3_NO_SSE41, BLAKE3_NO_AVX2 or BLAKE3_NO_AVX512 tells the dispatcher.
blake3_dispatch.c asks the CPU which it can run, once, and calls the best;
a CPU without any runs the portable code.  The upstream assembly files
(*.S, *.asm) are not used.

The functions keep upstream's names (blake3_hasher_*, blake3_hash_many_*
and so on), and the server exports them: a loadable module that bundles
its own BLAKE3 should build it with its symbols hidden, or it may bind to
these.  Upstream's assert()s stay on, as the server doesn't define
NDEBUG; they cost next to nothing.

To update: copy those files from the new release's c/ directory, make the
include change again, and check src/test/modules/test_prolly's BLAKE3
test vectors (and its check of each SIMD variant) still pass.
